<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Potidaea</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 356 BCE (approx.) · capital Potidaea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Corinthian colony on the isthmus of Pallene in Chalcidice whose revolt against Athens in 432 BCE helped trigger the Peloponnesian War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Potidaea was founded around 600 BCE by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a> under the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periander">Periander</a>, whose son Evagoras is named as founder. It stood on the narrow neck of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallene">Pallene</a> peninsula, controlling the way into it, and remained unusually close to its mother city, which still sent it annual magistrates in the 5th century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A walled port city with an assembly and magistrates, Potidaea lived from trade in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, Macedonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and the produce of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallene">Pallene</a>. It held out against the Persian commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artabazus-i-of-phrygia">Artabazus</a> in 480-479 BCE and fought at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/plataea">Plataea</a>. As a tribute-paying member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a> it was caught between loyalty to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> and its Corinthian ties.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ordered by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> to pull down its walls and expel the Corinthian magistrates, Potidaea revolted in 432 BCE and surrendered after a costly siege in 430 BCE; Athenian settlers replaced its people. It later joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcidian-league">Chalcidian League</a>, was retaken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 364 BCE and was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a> in 356 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> refounded it as Cassandreia.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/potidaea.jpg" alt="Potidaea" width="320" height="190"><figcaption>Image: William Robert Shepherd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chalcidice.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 BCE – 356 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Potidaea</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek (Doric)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Poteidaia, Cassandreia</dd></dl></aside></div>

